    "result": "`parse_line` is implemented in `solution.py` and the test suite in `tests/test_solution.py` passes (60 tests, `python -m pytest -q tests`). I only edited those two files and created nothing else.\n\nThe implementation validates arguments first: `TypeError` for non-`str` inputs, then `ValueError` for a `sep` that is not one character or is `\"`, CR or LF. The parser then walks the line field by field. A quoted field reads up to its closing quote, treating `\"\"` as one quote and allowing separators and line breaks inside, and it rejects any character other than `sep` right after the closing quote. An unquoted field runs to the next separator and rejects `\"`, CR or LF. Two small private helpers, `_read_quoted` and `_read_plain`, keep each function's complexity low. The tests cover every spec rule and every error path, and I checked by hand that each branch of `solution.py` is reached. I could not run coverage, CRAP, mutation or bandit checks here, because the shell is restricted to the test command.",
